menu "UI Component Configuration"

    config UI_DEEP_SLEEP_COUNTDOWN_SEC
        int "Deep sleep countdown duration (seconds)"
        default 60
        range 60 1800
        help
            Duration in seconds for the countdown timer from light sleep
            to deep sleep. The timer is armed by the moon tick on the
            LIGHT-SLEEP DUSK's last frame (the fully unilluminated side
            on screen, UI_MOON_DUSK_ENABLE) -- the sleep depth follows
            the animation, never a fixed timer at the sleep entry -- and
            triggers the transition to deep sleep when it expires.

            Recommended: 30-60 seconds for battery-powered devices.

    config UI_LIGHT_SLEEP_TICK_MS
        int "LVGL tick period in light sleep (milliseconds)"
        default 100
        range 50 1000
        help
            LVGL timer tick period when in light sleep mode.
            Slower tick reduces CPU usage and power consumption.
            Default is 100ms (vs 10ms in active mode).

    config UI_LIGHT_SLEEP_DIM_PCT
        int "Panel brightness in light sleep (percent)"
        default 25
        range 0 100
        help
            The panel's OWN hardware brightness (the CO5300 0x51 command /
            the P4's backlight PWM) while the light-sleep screen is shown:
            the entry ramps DOWN to this level (UI_SCREEN_SWITCH_FADE ms,
            the same pacing as the screen-switch fade) and the wake / an
            alarm ring ramps it back to 100. The dusk keeps spinning at
            this level (the whole display glows down, the moon included).
            The ramp follows the fade's panel-io rule: it takes the panel
            mutex per 0x51 step and holds the spin's panel writes
            (s_fade_busy), so a brightness write is never queued behind a
            strip DMA that could stall the bus (the settings-entry freeze).
            0 = the panel goes fully dark in light sleep (the wake fades
            it back in).

    config UI_CLOCK_ALPHA_PCT
        int "Clock opacity in light sleep (percent)"
        default 20
        range 0 100
        help
            The shared clock widget's LVGL opacity while the light-sleep
            screen is shown, as a percentage (0 = fully hidden, 100 =
            full opacity). The clock is drawn over the panel with this
            "alpha" -- a plain panel has no true alpha channel, so the
            clock's own opacity IS the alpha. Light sleep ALSO dims the
            whole panel to UI_LIGHT_SLEEP_DIM_PCT via the hardware
            brightness; this option dims the clock further relative to
            the moon (e.g. 20 = the clock glows at 20 % over the already
            dimmed panel, the original "barely visible" look). The wake /
            an alarm ring restores the clock to full opacity. Deep sleep
            keeps the dead display (backlight off, clock hidden), so this
            option is light-sleep only. The single shared clock widget
            means one application point (the light-sleep entry) covers
            every moon-background clock display.

    config UI_DEMO_MODE
        bool "Demo mode: MM:SS clock + the moon screens on a rotation"
        default n
        help
            A showcase mode for the clock's motion: the shared clock
            widget shows MM:SS (the seconds roll through the per-digit
            odometer every second) instead of HH:MM, and the five moon
            screens (airplane, wifi, light sleep, deep sleep, alarm)
            auto-rotate every UI_DEMO_SCREEN_SEC seconds. The demo runs
            until the next reset: it holds the idle timer (every demo
            second counts as activity), skips the hourly on-demand WiFi
            sync, and ignores the sync-complete hand-off, so a recording
            run is never cut by a sleep transition or a radio wake. Off
            (default) in production builds.

    config UI_DEMO_SCREEN_SEC
        int "Demo mode: seconds per screen in the rotation"
        default 15
        range 5 600
        help
            How long the demo mode (UI_DEMO_MODE) shows each of the five
            moon screens before rotating to the next. 15 s is the default
            (75 s per full cycle); lower it for a faster rotation when
            recording.

    config UI_MOON_ANIM_ENABLE
        bool "Play the synodic boot sweep (new moon -> now) at boot"
        default y
        help
            At boot the moon starts on a BLACK screen and replays the
            current synodic cycle -- from the last NEW MOON (a dark
            disk) to now, at most 29.5 days -- in UI_MOON_ANIM_FRAMES
            frames, baked off screen and displayed as atomic whole
            frames on the same pipeline as the burn-in spin (one ~150 ms
            frame, no top-to-bottom wipe, the display stays live
            throughout). The sweep always starts dark (the first frames
            are nearly black, so the transition from the blank boot
            screen reads as the new moon waxing; no wipe)
            and ends on the current phase, and hands the burn-in spin
            (UI_MOON_SPIN_*) back at the exact on-screen pose. This is
            the BOOT animation only: waking from sleep (touch, tilt or
            an alarm dismiss) shows the current moon phase STATIC, with
            no animation.

    config UI_MOON_ANIM_FRAMES
        int "Synodic boot sweep frame count"
        default 1344
        range 5 4096
        help
            How many phase frames make up the boot sweep, evenly spaced
            from the last NEW MOON to the current phase (the span is the
            moon's current age this cycle, at most 29.53 days). The
            frame count is fixed, so the sweep wall time is fixed
            (~frames x per-frame bake+display) while the PHASE STEP
            shrinks as the cycle is younger: at 1344 (the default) the
            step is up to ~0.022 d/frame (~32 min) at an old moon. The
            count was 336 until 2026-11-07 -- at that density the
            per-frame illumination jump was visible as a stutter, so it
            was quadrupled; the wall time grew with it (~37 min S3
            measured 2026-11-08 at the native 400 px default bake,
            ~40 min P4 @650 px -- the display stays live and the sweep
            hands over to the spin when done). The intermediate
            frames are baked at UI_MOON_ANIM_BAKE_PX (see there); the
            final frame is always full MOON_PX. Lower = faster but
            coarser sweep; the display stays live either way. When the
            TF sweep cache (UI_MOON_TFCACHE) can serve the card, the
            intermediate frames are instead the whole hour list of the
            current cycle (one frame per cached hour, at most ~709) and
            this count applies only to the card-less path (no card /
            cache off / cache disabled this boot).

    config UI_MOON_ANIM_BAKE_PX
        int "Synodic boot sweep intermediate-frame bake resolution (px)"
        # Kconfig evaluates multiple default lines FIRST-MATCH-WINS, so the
        # conditional line must precede the unconditional fallback.
        default 650 if BOARD_P4
        default 400
        range 100 650
        help
            The on-screen size the boot sweep's INTERMEDIATE frames are
            baked at before being upscased (nearest-neighbor) to the full
            MOON_PX for display. Both boards default to their own MOON_PX
            (S3 400, P4 650) -- every intermediate frame baked at full
            resolution, pixel-identical to the static/spin bakes (no
            upsample softness at the terminator), at the cost of a longer
            sweep. Measured on the S3 336-frame sweep: 200 -> ~0.21
            s/frame (~2.4 min), 240 -> ~0.28 s (~2.8 min), 320 -> ~0.48 s
            (~4.0 min), 400 (= MOON_PX) -> ~0.72 s (~5.3 min). The P4
            default 650 is an EXTRAPOLATION (~1.5-2 s/frame -> ~8-11 min
            for 336 frames, unmeasured on the panel); if the boot sweep
            becomes too slow, drop it (e.g. 512) -- one Kconfig value.
            Values above the board's MOON_PX are clamped to it: a bake
            above native adds no display quality (the screen shows
            MOON_PX), and the sharpness comes from the MOON_ALBEDO_W
            texture oversampling, not supersampled bakes. The reduced-res
            frames are physically correct (the lighting/relief model is
            resolution-independent; only the screen sampling is coarser,
            MOON_PX/S x MOON_PX/S blocks), which is acceptable in the fast
            montage. The last frame and the static/spin phase are ALWAYS
            full resolution regardless of this setting. (The light-sleep
            dusk does not bake intermediate frames at all: its constant-
            projection re-renders at full resolution, and its final
            dark-side frame is a full-resolution bake.) The TF sweep
            cache (UI_MOON_TFCACHE) keys its entries on the SAME bake
            size: the folder name and the self-describing meta.txt state
            it, and switching this value wipes the folder rather than
            resampling stale frames.

    config UI_MOON_TFCACHE
        bool "Cache boot-sweep frames on the TF card"
        default y
        help
            Persist the sweep's intermediate bakes on the TF card (one
            small gray JPEG per HOUR of the cycle, e.g. 709 files ~10 MB
            for a full month) under /moon_cache/<calcver>_<target>_<px>/,
            so a warm replay reads frames instead of computing them:
            the sweep wall time drops from minutes to the display
            cadence itself (~150 ms/frame). The folder is versioned by
            the calculation (MOON_CALC_VER from git describe; bump the
            repo with commitizen `cz bump` to invalidate), and a
            self-describing meta.txt inside it pins the effective BAKE
            RESOLUTION (plus albedo width / relief / JPEG quality): any
            mismatch -- above all switching UI_MOON_ANIM_BAKE_PX --
            wipes and recreates the folder, so frames are never served
            at the wrong resolution. Old version folders are deleted on
            sight; entries older than one synodic cycle are evicted when
            new ones are written (the cache never grows past ~29.5 days).
            Write-through: every frame the device had to compute is
            stored, so the next boot within the cycle replays it.
            The device works WITHOUT a card: missing card, failed mount,
            undecodable file, foreign filesystem or a mid-run detach all
            fall back silently to computing every frame on-device (the
            original path, unchanged). Frames fetched from the card go
            through the same moon_gray565 tint LUT as computed ones --
            replay and compute are pixel-identical by construction.
            The final frame is ALWAYS the live full-MOON_PX bake of the
            current phase (with its sky-event occlusion), cached or not.

    config UI_MOON_TFCACHE_JPEG_Q
        int "TF cache JPEG quality (1-100)"
        depends on UI_MOON_TFCACHE
        default 10
        range 1 100
        help
            Quality of the gray JPEGs the sweep cache stores. 10 is the
            knob-demo recipe class (their 240x240 Earth frames sat at q
            ~10 / ~6 KB and looked great on a small round panel): tiny
            files (~8-15 KB at 400 px), fast encode, banding only under
            close inspection of the mare -- invisible at the sweep's
            ~150 ms cadence. Raise it for product review (30-60 doubles
            file size); the sweep display path decodes either.

    config UI_MOON_TFCACHE_PREFETCH_MIN_SLOTS
        int "TF cache prefetch queue floor (slots)"
        depends on UI_MOON_TFCACHE
        default 2
        range 1 12
        help
            The prefetch task decodes card frames into a PSRAM slot queue
            ahead of the display; the depth is DERIVED from the free
            PSRAM at sweep start (free / (bake_px^2 * 1.15 + 4 KiB),
            capped at 12, logged per boot). This is the floor: even a
            tight-memory boot tries this many slots (and falls back to
            full on-device baking if even the floor cannot be allocated,
            or if 12 slots leave no headroom -- the queue only ever
            covers the INTERMEDIATE frames at bake_px).

    config UI_MOON_ANIM_ON_WAKE
        bool "Re-bake the moon on every wake from sleep (testing)"
        default y
        help
            Obsolete: the synodic sweep (UI_MOON_ANIM_*) now plays at
            BOOT only (from a black screen); wakes show the current
            phase static. The old re-bake test hook is gone. Kept so
            existing sdkconfig files keep validating.

    config UI_MOON_DUSK_ENABLE
        bool "Light-sleep dusk: pole-axis spin to the fully unilluminated side"
        default y
        help
            When the device enters light sleep the moon (parked at the
            exact free-spin pose, seamless seam) spins about its POLE at
            the current phase -- 1 degree per frame, the shortest arc to
            the fully unilluminated view (at most a half turn, 14-95 s
            at the ~0.2 s/frame display pace) -- and the deep-sleep RTC
            countdown (UI_DEEP_SLEEP_COUNTDOWN_SEC) is armed on the
            LAST frame's display, so the sleep depth follows the
            animation. The spin is a CONSTANT PROJECTION, not a re-bake:
            the renderer's color depends only on the body-frame surface
            direction and the epoch's fixed sun (the camera basis never
            enters the color), so the fully-lit body-frame equirect map
            (864x432) is baked ONCE for the epoch (~1.5-2 s) and every
            intermediate frame re-projects that fixed map through the
            folded camera basis -- a gather-class pass (~50 ms), no
            per-frame shadow/lighting recompute, and no terminator
            flicker (a fixed surface point keeps its exact color).
            The FINAL (dark-side) frame is one full MOON_PX bake at the
            endpoint (pixel-exact lighting) and lingers on the sleep
            screens; the dark side keeps its contrast (earthshine +
            relief-lit rims, dark_zero stays 0). Waking from sleep shows
            the CURRENT phase static at the parked pose -- no
            animation; the burn-in spin resumes from exactly the pose
            the dusk started from.

    config UI_MOON_SPIN_ENABLE
        bool "Free spin: rotate the moon in place (phase-anchored)"
        default y
        help
            The current phase is baked once (the static frame) and then
            rotated in-plane on screen by a FREE SPIN (a nearest-neighbor
            gather, ~8 ms/frame at the full 400x400 render size -- no
            re-bake; the lighting re-bake happens once per day, the
            rotation is pure gather): a spin angle integrated per
            frame whose rate is smoothed toward a target. While the
            device is in use (touched or tilted within the settle
            window) the rate is the ACTIVE period below; once it
            settles the rate eases to the SLOW SETTLED period (default
            600 s/turn) -- the continuous slow rotation moves the lit
            pixels around the panel, preventing AMOLED burn-in.
            Picking the device up spins it faster; letting it settle
            slows it visibly back to the settled rate. Each frame is
            written STRAIGHT to the panel from the render task (core
            1) via a wrapped esp_lcd_panel_draw_bitmap (the 400x400
            moon area, one whole-moon-area write per frame, the panel's
            SPI bus lock serializing with LVGL's own flushes). A UI
            keep-mask (analytic seconds-arc ring band + a full-screen
            lv_snapshot of the active screen with the moon image
            hidden) protects every non-black UI pixel -- clock digits,
            the alarm row, icons -- so the spin composites the frame
            only where the UI is not. The spin no longer routes
            through LVGL's ~175 ms whole-panel re-render, so the
            touch core (LVGL, core 0) stays free and the spin can
            show more, finer frames at lower CPU. The spin runs on
            every screen that shows the moon and is paused (not
            stopped) while the tilt-driven orbit re-bake, the boot
            sweep, the dusk, or a fade leg owns the buffer.

    config UI_MOON_SPIN_PERIOD_S
        int "Active spin: seconds for one full turn (while in use)"
        default 300
        range 30 7200
        help
            The spin rate while the device is in use: one full in-plane
            rotation in this many seconds (the default 300 s = a turn
            every 5 minutes). After the last user activity (touch or
            tilt) the rate eases down toward the settled period over the
            settle window. Lower = a faster (more visible) spin while
            in use.

    config UI_MOON_SPIN_SETTLED_PERIOD_S
        int "Settled spin: seconds for one full turn (at rest)"
        default 600
        range 60 7200
        help
            The spin rate once the device has settled (no touch or
            tilt within the settle window): one full in-plane rotation
            in this many seconds (the default 600 s = a turn every
            10 minutes, half the active rate). The spin stays VISIBLE
            at rest -- it is the AMOLED burn-in protection, so it
            must keep moving the lit pixels; the old phase-true coast
            (one turn per synodic month) was effectively invisible and
            read as the moon freezing a minute before light sleep.
            The continuous slow rotation keeps guarding against
            burn-in while the device sits on a table.

    config UI_MOON_SPIN_FPS
        int "Spin frame rate: rendered/panel-written frames per second"
        default 8
        range 2 30
        help
            How many spin frames the render task writes to the panel
            per second. Each frame is one cheap nearest-neighbor
            rotate of the baked 400x400 phase (~8 ms) plus a direct
            QSPI write of the moon area (~30-40 ms of panel time,
            QSPI quad at 40 MHz) -- the frame is paced to this rate,
            so a higher value is smoother (a smaller angular step per
            displayed frame) at a higher core-1 duty. The rate is
            time-integrated, so a dropped frame is a bigger step,
            never a repeat or a stall. 8 fps at the 300 s active
            period steps the rim by ~0.15 deg/frame -- smooth.

    config UI_MOON_SPIN_SETTLE_S
        int "Spin settle window: seconds of idle before easing to the settled rate"
        default 10
        range 2 60
        help
            After this much time without user activity (touch or tilt)
            the spin rate begins easing from the active period down to
            the settled period. The rate change is exponential (time
            constant ~ this/3), so a few multiples of this window
            settle the spin fully. Larger = the moon keeps its active
            spin longer after use.

    config TEST_AUTO_WAKE_SEC
        int "Test: auto-wake from sleep after N seconds (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 3600
        help
            Test hook: while the device is in an idle (sleep) state,
            trigger the idle-wake path automatically after N seconds of
            being asleep -- no touch needed. Lets the wake-from-sleep
            transitions (WiFi reconnect, screen switch, moon
            re-bake/animation) be exercised repeatedly without touching
            the device. 0 (default) = off.

    config TEST_SETTINGS_DEMO_SEC
        int "Test: scripted settings run N seconds after boot (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 3600
        help
            Test hook: run the scripted settings flow N seconds after
            boot (waits out the boot sync first): settings hub ->
            pomodoro (set + save a 90-minute interval) -> alarm time
            (set + save 12:35 Mon-Fri, the range-arc default) ->
            light-sleep (set the
            idle timeout + countdown + animation flags + save) ->
            airplane, logging every transition, the scheduler /
            settings-store writes, and reading the alarm / settings
            files back. Headless acceptance path for the whole
            settings stack (state machine, persistence, scheduler
            math). 0 (default) = off.

    config TEST_SPIN_BANDS
        int "Test: spin writes per-strip gray bands instead of the moon (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 100
        help
            Seam diagnostic for the spin's direct panel write. When >0,
            the spin replaces the rotated moon (and the UI keep-mask
            overlay) with one distinct gray level per strip -- the 16
            strips of the moon area become 16 horizontal bands, darkest
            to brightest, so any glitch at a strip seam (a stray line, a
            shifted boundary, a row that does not land) is visible on a
            clean pattern with no moon texture or mask to confound it.
            The write path (same strips, same geometry, same panel
            mutex) is unchanged; only the content differs.
            Value 1 = the bands written with the normal spin geometry
            (400-wide strips at x=33). Value 2 = the same band pattern
            written as FULL-PANEL-WIDTH strips (x 0..465, 466 px/row --
            the LVGL flush geometry): the A/B that isolates the
            400-wide strip window as the seam-line cause (the black
            edge margins cover the arc-ring slivers at the mid-height
            edges for the duration of the test). 0 (default) = the
            normal moon.

    config TEST_WIFI_SCAN_SEC
        int "Test: open the WiFi settings screen N seconds after boot (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 3600
        help
            Test hook: N seconds after boot (waits out the boot sweep
            first), open the settings WiFi screen -- the one that
            starts the background AP scan (wifi_scan_begin) and
            renders the results as rows. Headless verification of the
            scan path (on the P4: the esp_hosted RPC scan through the
            remote C6, including its 5 GHz APs). Idle monitoring is
            turned off while the screen is up so the capture is not
            cut by the sleep transition. 0 (default) = off.

    config TEST_CPU_STATS_SEC
        int "Test: log per-task CPU % every second, N seconds after boot (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 3600
        help
            Test hook: N seconds after boot, log every task's share of
            total CPU time (FreeRTOS run-time stats) once per second
            for CONFIG_TEST_CPU_STATS_SEC seconds. Enables the FreeRTOS
            run-time stats tick (the hook's only runtime cost: a two-word
            read on every context switch). Also loads the settings hub
            once at N+1 s (after 1 s of home-screen numbers), so one
            capture shows the coast-spin CPU on the home screen and the
            settings-idle CPU on the hub. Headless CPU profiling aid;
            0 (default) = off.

    config TEST_ROLL_TILT_SEC
        int "Test: headless screen-orientation tilt script N seconds after boot (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 3600
        help
            Test hook: N seconds after boot, feed the real
            90-degree-step orientation pipeline (100 ms gravity
            sampling -> int4 quantization -> 1 s running mean ->
            quadrant decision -> the LVGL transform layer on all 12
            screen roots) synthetic screen-plane gravity for ~25 s:
            resting (seeds the settled reference) -> 90 deg CW tilt
            -> 180 -> 90 deg CCW -> back to rest, so the applied
            values (with the +90 deg base orientation) log
            +90 -> 0 -> -90 -> -180 -> +90 and each run through the
            full window + decision + transform path on the device --
            no physical tilt needed (exercises the PSRAM layer
            allocation and the centre pivot). The 1 s window lags the
            step changes by ~1 s. Idle monitoring is paused for the
            script so it does not dusk-sleep mid-run.
            0 (default) = off.

    config TEST_ALARM_RING_SEC
        int "Test: fire the alarm ring N seconds after the boot sweep (0 = off)"
        default 0
        range 0 3600
        help
            Test hook: N seconds after boot (waits out the boot sweep
            first), fire the REAL alarm path (action_alarm_triggered:
            the ring start + the alarm screen); ~9 s later (past the
            2.5 s volume ramp) stop the ring the way the touch dismiss
            does (the ~150 ms fade-out + codec close); re-fire it ~4 s
            later (the codec re-open after the close); ring again, stop
            it again, then finish with the real dismiss
            (action_wake_screen). On the P4 it proves the ES8311
            open/stream/ramp/fade/close/re-open cycle; on the S3 the
            ring functions are no-op stubs, so it exercises the
            alarm-screen path only. One state per second, in the LVGL
            task. 0 (default) = off.

    config TEST_HANG_WATCH
        bool "Test: dump every task's backtrace + abort when the LVGL task stalls (off = never)"
        default n
        help
            Test hook: a 200 ms lv_timer on the LVGL task is the
            heartbeat; a watcher task aborts the system (after printing
            every task's name/state and a symbolized backtrace) when the
            heartbeat is stale for more than 15 s. A hang on the LVGL
            task is otherwise SILENT (no WDT, no assert -- a task parked
            on a portMAX_DELAY lock keeps the CPU1 idle task alive, so
            the task WDT never fires and the console just goes quiet
            while the panel holds a half-drawn frame). 0 (default) =
            off; the hook is a pure diagnostic aid, not for production.

    config TEST_BAKE_NO_RELIEF
        bool "Test: bake without the relief gradient cache (perf A/B)"
        default n
        help
            Test hook: pass a NULL relief cache to every bake (relief-free
            L = clip(d, 0, 1)) to measure how much of the per-frame bake
            cost is the 2 relief-cache PSRAM loads + tangent-basis dots
            vs the common path (base direction, texture tables, albedo
            load, earthshine, tonemap). Temporary profiling aid; off by
            default and visually wrong (flat terminator).

    config UI_IMU_ENABLE
        bool "Drive the moon viewport from the QMI8658 IMU (device tilt)"
        default y
        help
            The moon's in-plane pose is the free spin's (UI_MOON_SPIN_*,
            which rotates the baked phase in place); the device tilt
            (QMI8658 gravity) changes which side of the sphere the
            camera sees (the out-of-plane orbit, see UI_MOON_ORBIT_*).
            Tilt also counts as user activity: it holds the idle timer,
            wakes the device from sleep, and drives the spin's active
            rate (picking the device up spins it faster). Touch is not
            an input to the moon's pose (it is only the core-UI wake
            input). When the IMU is absent or this is disabled the
            orbit is off (the tilt monitor runs on but gets no pose
            input, so no tilt-driven activity/wake; the free spin still
            runs when UI_MOON_SPIN_ENABLE is set).

    config UI_IMU_ACTIVITY_DEG
        int "Tilt activity threshold: departure from the settled pose (degrees)"
        default 5
        range 1 45
        help
            The monitor measures the DEPARTURE (deg) of the current
            pose from a settled-pose reference (a slow EMA that only
            updates while settled), not the delta between two raw
            samples (which amplifies per-sample noise and fired the
            "wakes right after entering sleep" bug). While the device
            is AWAKE, a departure above this threshold counts as user
            activity: it holds the idle timer, so a device operated
            purely by tilt cannot be stranded asleep. At rest the
            departure settles back below it and the reference follows,
            so a still device is free to sleep. A two-consecutive-
            sample streak gates the fire.

    config UI_IMU_WAKE_DEG
        int "Tilt wake threshold: departure from the settled pose (degrees)"
        default 20
        range 5 45
        help
            While the device is ASLEEP, a departure above this
            (deliberate-pick-up) threshold wakes it, gated by a
            two-consecutive-sample streak. Higher than
            UI_IMU_ACTIVITY_DEG on purpose: table vibration and the
            measured at-rest drift (~0.4 deg/s -> ~2.5 deg of steady
            departure) must not wake, only a deliberate tilt. Lower it
            for more sensitive wake.

    config UI_IMU_ANGLE_SIGN
        int "IMU angle sign (+1 or -1, flips the orbit direction)"
        default 1
        range -1 1
        help
            The board's IMU mounting orientation is board-specific; if
            tilting the device peeks the moon the "wrong" way, set this
            to -1. Applies to the orbit (UI_MOON_ORBIT_*) only: the
            tilt activity/wake monitor measures a DEPARTURE (an angle
            from the settled pose), so it is sign-agnostic.

    config UI_IMU_ROLL_OFFSET_DEG
        int "IMU roll offset (degrees, aligns the resting orientation)"
        default 0
        range 0 359
        help
            Obsolete: the free spin (UI_MOON_SPIN_*) is time-driven,
            not tilt-driven, so a resting roll offset has no effect.
            Kept so existing sdkconfig files keep validating.

    config UI_CLOCK_ROLL_SIGN
        int "Screen-orientation correction sign (+1 or -1)"
        default 1
        range -1 1
        help
            The screen-plane gravity vector (the IMU x/y gravity) picks
            the WHOLE-SCREEN orientation: the UI content counter-rotates
            (lv_obj_set_style_transform_rotation on every screen root)
            in 90-degree steps so the UI stays upright relative to the
            resting pose, on the clock, the settings and the alarm
            screens alike (with the orbit, UI_MOON_ORBIT_ENABLE, off on
            the S3 this is the tilt's only UI coupling). The gravity
            x/y is sampled at 10 Hz, a 1 s running mean is the 1 Hz
            low-pass, both axes are quantized to int4, and the
            orientation is the departure of the filtered gravity vector
            from a settled-pose reference (updated only while the
            device is settled, so at-rest drift does not walk the
            orientation around), decided with integer dot/cross only
            (no atan2). Only applied when the screen plane has a
            gravity component (near-vertical or flat the pose is
            undefined and the last orientation is kept). The resting
            orientation (0) costs nothing -- LVGL takes its direct draw
            path -- so the PSRAM transform layer is only paid while the
            device sits in a tilted 90-degree pose. The board's IMU
            mounting orientation may flip the measured tilt; if the UI
            rotates the WRONG way when the device is tilted, set this
            to -1.

    config UI_ORI_BASE_V10
        int "Base screen orientation in 1/10 deg (panel mount offset)"
        # Per-board panel mount offset, first-match-wins order (the
        # moon asset-res precedent): the P4's panel is mounted 90 deg
        # CW off (tuned on the bench, task #54); the S3 AMOLED housing
        # needs a further quarter turn clockwise (bench 2026-11-08: at
        # rest its content sat 90 deg CCW of true up).
        default 900 if BOARD_P4
        default 1800
        range 0 2700
        help
            The whole-screen rotation at orientation step 0 (the pose
            the settled-pose reference anchors to): positive =
            clockwise, LVGL transform units (1/10 deg). Applied at
            init and on every 2 s press re-sync, and it feeds the
            spin's direct-panel pre-rotation and UI keep-mask through
            the pushed value - this is the single source for the mount
            offset. Must be a multiple of 900: the spin's step table
            only understands quarter turns.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_ENABLE
        bool "Sphere model: peek at the dark side via out-of-plane tilt"
        default n
        help
            The moon behaves like a sphere: the out-of-plane tilt of the
            device (the angle between the screen normal and vertical,
            0 deg at vertical / wall-mounted, 90 deg flat on a table)
            rotates the camera around the moon, so the dark side can be
            peeked at -- on a near-full moon this reveals the
            unilluminated limb. This requires a re-bake with a
            pre-rotated camera basis (~1.7-2.4 s with the relief DEM
            on this board); the finished frame is then displayed as one
            atomic whole frame, exactly like the burn-in spin (one
            ~150 ms frame -- the same sequential update the spin uses),
            so the viewport change lands as a frame instead of a slow
            wipe. The free spin (UI_MOON_SPIN_*) rotates the baked
            frame in place on top of the orbit's viewport.

            DISABLED by default: the ~2 s relief-DEM re-bake cannot be
            served at the tilt-driven cadence on the ESP32-S3 (the
            display pipeline stalls and the viewport lags the tilt), so
            on the S3 the tilt drives the CLOCK orientation instead
            (UI_CLOCK_ROLL_SIGN) and the moon stays on its default
            viewport. The in-plane free spin and the tilt activity/wake
            monitor are unaffected. Re-enable on the ESP32-P4, which can
            hold the re-bake rate.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_MAX_DEG
        int "Max orbit (peek) angle in degrees"
        default 30
        range 0 60
        help
            The viewport only moves by the tilt up to this limit:
            beyond it the orbit is clamped. 0 disables the orbit
            (the moon stays on its default viewport regardless of
            out-of-plane tilt). A wall-mounted device reads ~0 deg
            (vertical = the default viewport); flat on a table it
            reads 90 deg, which clamps to this limit.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_STEP_DEG
        int "Orbit quantization step in degrees (re-bake trigger)"
        default 1
        range 1 10
        help
            The orbit angle is quantized to this step and the re-bake
            only runs when the quantized target moves. 1 degree is the
            default (no visible stutter at rest); raise it (e.g. 3-5)
            if the ~2.3 s re-bake makes viewport changes feel too
            slow for the device's re-draw performance.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_DEADZONE_DEG
        int "Orbit dead zone in degrees"
        default 2
        range 0 15
        help
            Quantized orbit angles inside +/- this band map to the
            default viewport (0), so table jitter (at-rest floor
            measured at ~1.5 deg/s mean, 3.3 deg/s p90 at 1 Hz on
            this board) cannot walk the viewport around.

    config UI_MOON_ORBIT_LPF_ALPHA
        int "Orbit low-pass filter coefficient in percent (5-95)"
        default 30
        range 5 95
        help
            Exponential smoothing on the orbit angle, applied at the
            20 Hz UI tick, as a percentage: 30 = alpha 0.30 per tick.
            Together with the dead zone and the quantization step this
            eliminates sensor stutter; raise it for a snappier
            (jitterier) response, lower it for a heavier low-pass.

    config UI_SCREEN_SWITCH_FADE
        int "Screen switch: fade-to-black time in ms (0 = plain atomic switch)"
        default 240
        range 0 2000
        help
            A screen switch (e.g. into the settings hub) dims the
            panel's OWN hardware brightness (the CO5300 0x51 command)
            to black, loads the new screen, displays it as ONE atomic
            whole-panel flush while the panel is still black, then
            ramps the brightness back. The transition writes NO
            pixels -- no overlay, no per-step re-render, no strip
            traffic on the panel bus -- so it is cheap and shows no
            left-to-right crawl. The total switch cost is roughly 2x
            this time (the two ramps) plus one hidden whole-panel
            flush. 0 = the plain instant atomic switch (lv_scr_load
            + one whole-panel flush). EVERY screen switch takes the
            fade; the one exception is the both-moon switch (airplane
            / wifi / the sleep screens share the one moon image),
            which stays atomic.

endmenu
